<Description contentType="preformatted" classID="C4ECC6B6-1A5E-41D8-97DE-807334D22D07">
  <Abstract>&lt;Content&gt;&lt;Paragraph&gt;The function &lt;Replace key="EnclosingFunction.name"/&gt; in &lt;Replace key="PrimaryLocation.file"/&gt; uses a cryptographic encryption algorithm with an insecure mode of operation on line &lt;Replace key="PrimaryLocation.line"/&gt;.&lt;AltParagraph&gt;Do not use cryptographic encryption algorithms with an insecure mode of operation.&lt;/AltParagraph&gt;&lt;/Paragraph&gt;&lt;/Content&gt;</Abstract>
  <Explanation>&lt;Content&gt;The mode of operation of a block cipher is an algorithm that describes how to repeatedly apply a cipher's single-block operation to securely transform amounts of data larger than a block. Some modes of operation include Electronic Codebook (ECB), Cipher Block Chaining (CBC), Cipher Feedback (CFB), and Counter (CTR).

ECB mode is inherently weak, as it produces the same ciphertext for identical blocks of plain text. CBC mode is vulnerable to padding oracle attacks. CTR mode is the superior choice because it does not have these weaknesses.

&lt;b&gt;Example 1:&lt;/b&gt; The following code uses the AES cipher with ECB mode:

&lt;pre&gt;
...
SecretKeySpec key = new SecretKeySpec(keyBytes, "AES");
Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/ECB/PKCS7Padding", "BC");
cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, key);
...
&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/Content&gt;</Explanation>
  <Recommendations>&lt;Content&gt;Avoid using ECB and CBC modes of operation when encrypting data larger than a block. CBC mode is somewhat inefficient and poses a serious risk if used with SSL [1]. Instead, use CCM (Counter with CBC-MAC) mode or, if performance is a concern, GCM (Galois/Counter Mode) mode where they are available.

&lt;b&gt;Example 2:&lt;/b&gt; The following code uses the AES cipher with GCM mode:

&lt;pre&gt;
...
SecretKeySpec key = new SecretKeySpec(keyBytes, "AES");
Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/GCM/PKCS5Padding", "BC");
cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, key);
...
&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/Content&gt;</Recommendations>
  <References>
    <Reference>
      <Title>CVE 2014-3566</Title>
      <Source>http://web.nvd.nist.gov/view/vuln/detail?vulnId=CVE-2014-3566</Source>
    </Reference>
    <Reference>
      <Title>An Empirical Study of Cryptographic Misuse in Android Applications</Title>
      <Author>Manuel Egele, David Brumley, Yanick Fratantonio, Christopher Kruegel</Author>
      <PublishedDate>2013</PublishedDate>
      <Source>https://sites.cs.ucsb.edu/~chris/research/doc/ccs13_cryptolint.pdf</Source>
    </Reference>
    <Reference>
      <Title>CWE ID 327</Title>
      <Author>Standards Mapping - Common Weakness Enumeration</Author>
    </Reference>
    <Reference>
      <Title>CCI-002450</Title>
      <Author>Standards Mapping - DISA Control Correlation Identifier Version 2</Author>
    </Reference>
    <Reference>
      <Title>MP</Title>
      <Author>Standards Mapping - FIPS200</Author>
    </Reference>
    <Reference>
      <Title>Insufficient Data Protection</Title>
      <Author>Standards Mapping - General Data Protection Regulation</Author>
    </Reference>
    <Reference>
      <Title>SC-13 Cryptographic Protection (P1)</Title>
      <Author>Standards Mapping - NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 4</Author>
    </Reference>
    <Reference>
      <Title>SC-13 Cryptographic Protection</Title>
      <Author>Standards Mapping - NIST Special Publication 800-53 Revision 5</Author>
